Subject: Making `%+%` more indulgent (i.e. not breaking previous definitions of the same operator)
Message-ID: <2nDyX38m8uc.ljzFcMQbBgAJ@ggplot2.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/ggplot2/c/2nDyX38m8uc/m/ljzFcMQbBgAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Right now `%+%` is defined in such a way that it calls `+.gg` if the first argument is `theme` or `ggplot`, otherwise just returns NULL. If `%+%` was previously defined in another package, loading `ggplot2` package will break it - but I don't see why this is necessary if they work with non-overlapping classes. I tried changing the following in the `plot-construction.r` source file: instead of definition `"%+%" <- `+.gg`` (line 54 <https://github.com/tidyverse/ggplot2/blob/master/R/plot-construction.r#L54>), I put the following:
"%+%" <- function(...) UseMethod("%+%")
"%+%.theme" <- `+.gg`
"%+%.ggplot" <- `+.gg`
(of course don't forget adding corresponding `export()` instructions to the `NAMESPACE`)

I have built and loaded this fork and it works as expected: now I can load another package (where `%+%` is defined to work with `character` class), then load `ggplot2`, and they both work correctly. Is there any special reason of not implementing this?

P.S. I have asked this question (in more extended way) at StackOverflow - you can also comment or answer there <http://stackoverflow.com/questions/41786533/>.

